t":" set diversion time of a"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" suitable airport."},{"type":"text_delta","text":" The Arctic has few"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" airports, so air"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"lines need appro"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ved di"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"version plans.\n- **"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"Cold and"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" rad"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"iation.**"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" Fuel must st"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ay above"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" its"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" freezing point at"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" very"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" low temperatures. Cr"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ews also mon"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"itor solar radiation stor"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ms, which can dis"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"rupt communications and nav"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"igation near the po"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"les and force a"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" lower-lat"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"itude route"},{"type":"text_delta","text":".\n- **Nav"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"igation and"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" communications.** Mag"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"netic comp"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"asses are"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" unreliable near the p"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ole, and some"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" satellite co"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"verage is limited."},{"type":"text_delta","text":" Mod"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ern a"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ircraft r"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ely on GPS and in"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ertial navigation,"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" and on"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" sat"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ellite or"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" HF rad"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"io.\n\nI"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" answ"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ered this"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" from general"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" knowledge and"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" didn't search the web."},{"type":"text_delta","text":" I"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" can look"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" up current rout"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ings"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" or fl"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ight times for"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" a specific airline if you want"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"."},{"type":"completed","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"opaque","provider":"anthropic","kind":"thinking","data":{"signature":"","thinking":"","type":"thinking"}},{"type":"text","text":"Planes fly polar routes between Toronto and Asia because the shortest path between the two is over the Arctic.\n\n**Great-circle geometry.** The shortest path between two points on a sphere is a great circle. On a flat map (Mercator) that path looks curved and northward, but it is the straight-line route on the globe. Toronto to Hong Kong, Tokyo, Seoul or Shanghai all pass far north of the straight line you'd draw on a flat map. Routes to northern and eastern Asia go over northern Canada, the Arctic Ocean and Russia. Going \"across the Pacific\" would be longer.\n\n**Savings.** A polar route can cut hundreds of kilometres and an hour or more from the trip compared with a route over the Pacific or the western US. That means less fuel, lower cost, less CO₂ and shorter travel times. For example, Toronto to Hong Kong is about 15–16 hours.\n\n**Winds.** Airlines pick routes day by day to use tailwinds and avoid headwinds, which can matter as much as distance. Polar routes often have good winds, especially for the eastbound or westbound direction that suits the day's jet stream position.\n\n**Constraints that shape these flights:**\n- **Airspace.** Since 2022, many Western carriers can't cross Russian airspace. Air Canada and others have had to reroute some Asia flights, which makes them longer and sometimes less \"polar\".\n- **Twin-engine rules (ETOPS/EDTO).** Aircraft must stay within a set diversion time of a suitable airport. The Arctic has few airports, so airlines need approved diversion plans.\n- **Cold and radiation.** Fuel must stay above its freezing point at very low temperatures. Crews also monitor solar radiation storms, which can disrupt communications and navigation near the poles and force a lower-latitude route.\n- **Navigation and communications.** Magnetic compasses are unreliable near the pole, and some satellite coverage is limited. Modern aircraft rely on GPS and inertial navigation, and on satellite or HF radio.\n\nI answered this from general knowledge and didn't search the web. I can look up current routings or flight times for a specific airline if you want."}]},"stop_reason":"end_turn","usage":{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":841,"cache_read_input_tokens":4517,"cache_creation_input_tokens":93},"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","refusal_category":null}]}
